lessons.syntax:
```python
for item in sequence:
    if skip_this_one:
        continue       # jump to the next round
    if finished:
        break          # leave the loop completely
    do_work()          # only reached when neither happened

while True:            # loops forever on purpose...
    line = input()
    if line == "stop":
        break          # ...until break ends it
```

`break` and `continue` almost always sit inside an `if`. `while True:` with a `break` is the usual way to write "read until a stop word".

