lessons.why:
Anything with rows and columns needs two loops: seats in a cinema, cells in a table, every pairing of two lists of options. One loop walks the rows. Inside it, another walks the columns of that row.

```python
for row in range(1, 3):
    for seat in range(1, 4):
        print(row, seat)
```

